<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/chief-seattle" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chief-seattle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-26">26 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">A coalition of Native American tribes attacks the settlement of Seattle</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Native American tribes attacked Seattle, a four-year-old settlement in Washington Territory that had named itself after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chief-seattle">Chief Seattle</a>, a leader of the Suquamish and Duwamish peoples. Marines from the USS Decatur drove off the attackers after a day of fighting.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chief-seattle">Chief Seattle</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crimean-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-30">30 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Treaty of Paris ends the Crimean War</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> accepted the neutralisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, barring warships from its waters, and gave up southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>. The powers guaranteed the integrity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/juan-santamaria" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-santamaria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-11">11 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Costa Rican militia defeat William Walker at the Second Battle of Rivas</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Costa Rican militia under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-rafael-mora-porras">Juan Rafael Mora Porras</a> fought the Nicaraguan forces of the American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a>. The drummer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-santamaria">Juan Santamaría</a> died carrying a torch to set the enemy stronghold on fire, and became the national hero of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-santamaria">Juan Santamaría</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/paris" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paris.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-16">16 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Powers sign the Paris Declaration on maritime law</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Meeting at the Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, the great powers agreed to abolish privateering and protect neutral goods at sea. It was the first multilateral treaty codifying the laws of naval warfare.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-22">22 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Senator Charles Sumner is beaten on the floor of the Senate</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Congressman Preston Brooks attacked the anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> senator with a cane after Sumner's speech against slavery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>. The assault deepened the bitterness between North and South.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-24">24 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">John Brown and his men kill five pro-slavery settlers in Kansas</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">After pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> forces sacked Lawrence, Brown and a band of abolitionist settlers abducted and murdered five pro-slavery settlers near Pottawatomie <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a> Territory, in the night of May 24–25. A teenage son of one settler was spared.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-brown-abolitionist">John Brown</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-2">2 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Tianjing Incident tears apart the Taiping leadership</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">In Tianjing, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> Heavenly Kingdom, a conflict among its leaders killed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-xiuqing">East King</a> Yang Xiuqing, the North King Wei Changhui and more than 27,000 others. It is said to have been the turning point toward the rebellion's failure.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-xiuqing">Yang Xiuqing</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-8">8 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Chinese officials board the Arrow at Canton</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Qing officials boarded the Arrow, a Chinese-owned ship flying a British flag, and arrested its crew on suspicion of piracy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> used the incident as grounds for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-opium-war">Second Opium War</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sabana-larga">Battle of Sabana Larga</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Sabana Larga was fought on 24 January 1856 near Dajabón in the north-west of the Dominican Republic.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ndondakusuka">Battle of Ndondakusuka</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Ndondakusuka was fought on 2 December 1856 near the Thukela River between two sons of the Zulu king Mpande competing for the succession.</p></div></li></ol></section>
